[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for “fly-to” navigation that feels smooth, stays on-brand with cartographic best-practice, and works on any MapLibre GL JS map (v2.x or later).

---

## 1. What “fly” really means in MapLibre

| Method | What it does | When to use it |
|--------|--------------|----------------|
| `map.flyTo(options)` | Animates the **center, zoom, bearing, pitch** together using a “smooth-flight” easing curve (the classic “fly-to” you see in Mapbox/MapLibre demos). | Most UI actions – button click, menu selection, search result, “go to my location”. |
| `map.easeTo(options)` | Same parameters but uses a **linear-ease** (no “overshoot” effect). Good for subtle moves (e.g., panning a few kilometres). | Minor adjustments, UI that should feel “tight”. |
| `map.jumpTo(options)` | Instantly snaps to the new view (no animation). | When you need an immediate change (e.g., resetting after a loading error). |

**Fly-to** is the default for “smooth navigation” because it keeps the user oriented: the camera accelerates, then decelerates, and the path follows a great-circle arc on the globe (or a straight line on a flat Mercator).  

---

## 2. Minimal HTML & CSS

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre Fly-to Demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .control {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      padding: 8px 12px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div class="control" id="flyBtn">✈️ Fly to Paris</div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="fly.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*The button (`#flyBtn`) is a simple, high-contrast control that works on both light and dark basemaps. If you are using an **imagery** basemap, give the button a dark-transparent background (`rgba(0,0,0,0.6)`) so it stays readable.*

---

## 3. JavaScript – the “fly” logic (`fly.js`)

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Initialise the map (any style you like)
// ---------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // light vector style
  center: [-0.1276, 51.5074], // London
  zoom: 10,
  pitch: 0,
  bearing: 0,
  // If you use satellite imagery, remember the halo rules from the cartography skill:
  //   text-color: "#ffffff"
  //   text-halo-color: "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)"
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Helper: a reusable fly-to function
// ---------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Fly the camera to a target location.
 *
 * @param {Array<number>} lngLat   – [lon, lat] in EPSG:4326
 * @param {Object}   opts          – optional overrides (zoom, bearing, pitch, duration)
 */
function flyToLocation(lngLat, opts = {}) {
  const defaultOpts = {
    center: lngLat,
    zoom: 12,               // a comfortable city-level zoom
    bearing: 0,             // north-up; change if you want a rotated view
    pitch: 45,              // 0-60° gives a 3-D feel without losing label legibility
    speed: 1.2,             // speed multiplier (default 1.2)
    curve: 1.42,            // curvature of the flight (default 1.42)
    easing: t => t,         // linear easing – you can replace with d3-ease for custom feel
    duration: 2000,         // ms – 2 seconds feels smooth on desktop & mobile
    essential: true         // required for accessibility (see note below)
  };

  // Merge user overrides
  const flyOpts = Object.assign({}, defaultOpts, opts);

  // Run the animation
  map.flyTo(flyOpts);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Wire the button click
// ---------------------------------------------------
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  // Paris – Eiffel Tower (you can replace with any lon/lat)
  const paris = [2.2945, 48.8584];
  flyToLocation(paris, {
    zoom: 14,
    pitch: 60,          // a bit more dramatic for the landmark
    bearing: 30,        // rotate slightly to showcase the tower
    duration: 2500      // a tad longer for the extra pitch change
  });
});
```

### Why these options?

| Option | Reason (cartographic best-practice) |
|--------|--------------------------------------|
| `zoom: 12-14` | Keeps **label readability** – at zoom 12 the city name is still legible, at 14 you can see street-level POI labels. |
| `pitch: 45-60` | Adds depth without breaking the **halo-contrast rule** for labels (the halo still surrounds the text because the map is still 2-D). |
| `bearing` | Small rotations (≤ 45°) preserve the **figure-ground relationship**; large rotations can disorient users. |
| `speed` & `curve` | The defaults (`speed: 1.2`, `curve: 1.42`) give a natural-looking flight. Tweak `speed` down for a slower, more “tour-guide” feel, or up for a quick jump. |
| `duration` | Explicit duration overrides the speed-based calculation, guaranteeing the same timing on all devices. |
| `essential: true` | Tells assistive-technology that the animation is **non-essential** for understanding the map content (WCAG 2.1). Users who have “prefers-reduced-motion” set will have the animation suppressed automatically. |

---

## 4. Respecting **prefers-reduced-motion**

If a user has enabled the OS-level “reduce motion” setting, you should **skip the animation** to avoid motion sickness.

```js
function flyToLocation(lngLat, opts = {}) {
  const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  const base = {
    center: lngLat,
    zoom: 12,
    bearing: 0,
    pitch: 45,
    speed: 1.2,
    curve: 1.42,
    duration: 2000,
    essential: true
  };
  const flyOpts = Object.assign(base, opts);

  if (prefersReduced) {
    // Jump instantly – no animation
    map.jumpTo(flyOpts);
  } else {
    map.flyTo(flyOpts);
  }
}
```

---

## 5. Adding a **fallback label** for screen-reader users

Because the map canvas itself isn’t readable, provide a hidden but accessible description that updates when you fly.

```html
<p id="mapDesc" class="sr-only" aria-live="polite"></p>
```

```css
/* Visually hidden but still read by AT */
.sr-only {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px; height: 1px;
  padding: 0; margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0,0,0,0);
  white-space: nowrap; border: 0;
}
```

```js
function updateMapDescription(lngLat, name) {
  const desc = `Map moved to ${name} (longitude ${lngLat[0].toFixed(4)}, latitude ${lngLat[1].toFixed(4)}).`;
  document.getElementById('mapDesc').textContent = desc;
}

// In the click handler:
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  const paris = [2.2945, 48.8584];
  flyToLocation(paris, { zoom: 14, pitch: 60, bearing: 30 });
  updateMapDescription(paris, 'Paris, France');
});
```

Now a screen-reader will announce the new location as soon as the animation starts (or jumps).

